At $m\ne0$ the formula is $0/0$, so \cref{eq:zeta-cont} by itself does not tell us the value of $\zeta$ there.

\begin{beyond}
\textbf{$\zeta$ is finite at $s=1+2\pi i m/\log2$, $m\ne0$.} In this section we take on trust that $\zeta$ has no pole at these points (equivalently, that $\eta$ vanishes there, so the $0/0$ has a finite limit). The book does not leave this unproved: Problems 25 and 26 construct a second continuation $\zeta=\eta_3/(1-3^{1-s})$ whose denominator vanishes at different points, which shows that $\zeta$ is finite at every point of $\Re s>0$ except $s=1$. Once that is known, \cref{eq:zeta-etazeta}, which holds on all of $\Re s>0$ by uniqueness of continuation, gives $\eta=(1-2^{1-s})\zeta=0$ at these points. A direct treatment is in \cite{titchmarsh}, Chapter~2.
\end{beyond}

This is the logic of the large verifications, and \texttt{mpmath.zetazero} locates zeros with the same ingredients: sign changes of $Z$, organized with the help of the counting function, followed by refinement.

\begin{beyond}
\textbf{Making the count rigorous.} Turning the comparison above into a proof requires an exact, certified value of $N(T)$ at the chosen heights. A method due to A.~M.~Turing (1953) provides a checkable way to confirm the count using values of $Z$ alone. We take this method on trust; it is described in \cite{edwards}.
\end{beyond}
